# <PROJECT> — what a generated hardware repo keeps and what it regenerates.
#
# COPY THIS INTO YOUR PROJECT AT PHASE 0, before the first export.  The rule
# it encodes is not "keep the repo small", it is: the repo is a DESCRIPTION OF
# THE DESIGN plus the artifacts you actually ordered.  Everything else is
# regenerable from `make`, and a directory of regenerable files is a directory
# someone will eventually mistake for the source of truth.
#
# The recommended answer, and why:
#   * COMMIT the upload zip and the BOM.  The zip is the thing that was
#     ordered, and `gerber_diff.py` needs an "old" side to prove a
#     regeneration did not change the copper.
#   * COMMIT the board and schematic files.  They are generated, but they are
#     also the artifact the gate ran against; keeping them is what makes
#     "committed artifact still passes, fresh regeneration does not" a usable
#     toolchain-regression test.
#   * IGNORE the loose intermediates and every generated report.
#
# Write this file BEFORE the first export.  Doing it after a cleanup pass is
# the one phase where it cannot help: the deletions come straight back.

# --- python -----------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# --- generated gate reports -------------------------------------------------
erc.json
drc.json
drc-all.json
.digest-before
.digest-after

# --- KiCad per-user state (never the project's own settings) -----------------
*.kicad_prl
*-backups/
fp-info-cache
*.lck

# --- fab package: keep the zip and the BOM, drop the intermediates ----------
fab/*/*.gbr
fab/*/*.gbrjob
fab/*/*.drl
fab/*/*-drl_map.pdf
fab/*/*-drill-report.txt
fab/*/*-pos-*.csv
# kept deliberately:
!fab/*/*-gerbers.zip
!fab/*/*-bom.csv
!fab/*/*-manifest.txt

# --- renders and plots: cheap to regenerate, noisy in a diff ----------------
review/

# --- enclosure --------------------------------------------------------------
# The venv is local to the machine; the exported solids are deliverables, so
# they are NOT ignored here.  Ignore them instead if the print files are
# published somewhere else.
case/.venv/
